<article class="help-article p-4">
    <header class="help-article-header mb-4">
        <span class="material-symbols-outlined help-article-icon">create_new_folder</span>
        <h1 class="help-article-title">Create Your First Project</h1>
        <p class="help-article-lead">
            A project is the top of the Singularity object graph. Every model, entity, and generated
            file belongs to a project, which is backed by a folder on your disk.
        </p>
    </header>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Create a new project</h2>
    <ol class="help-steps">
        <li>
            <strong>Open the Welcome window.</strong>
            It appears when you launch Singularity and lists your existing projects.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Choose a location.</strong>
            Click <em>Browse&hellip;</em> and pick (or create) the folder where the project will live.
            The folder's name becomes the project name.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Pick your generation options.</strong>
            Decide whether the generated service should include security, CORS, and JWT support.
            These can be changed later.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Click <em>Create</em>.</strong>
            Singularity scaffolds the project on disk and opens it in the Editor.
        </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="help-callout">
        <span class="material-symbols-outlined">folder_open</span>
        <div>
            <strong>Opening an existing project.</strong>
            To reopen a project you created earlier, choose its folder with <em>Open&hellip;</em> instead.
            Singularity reads the model back from the folder.
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="help-heading">What gets created</h2>
    <p>
        A new project starts with an empty <strong>model</strong>. From here you'll add entities to shape
        your data. Continue with
        <a class="help-link" href="#entities" data-help-anchor="entities">Working with Entities</a>.
    </p>
</article>
